  cursor: pointer; padding: 4px; line-height: 1; font-size: 12px; border-radius: 4px; flex-shrink: 0; }
.wehax-toast .toast-x:hover { color: var(--text); background: var(--bg-0); }
.wehax-toast .toast-progress {
  position: absolute; bottom: 0; left: 0; height: 2px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--brand), var(--cyan));
  width: 100%; transform-origin: left center;
  animation: toastProgress linear forwards;
}
.wehax-toast.ok   .toast-progress { background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--green),  var(--cyan)); }
.wehax-toast.warn .toast-progress { background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--amber),  var(--orange)); }
.wehax-toast.crit .toast-progress { background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--red),    var(--orange)); }
@keyframes toastProgress { from { transform: scaleX(1); } to { transform: scaleX(0); } }
.wehax-toast.leaving { animation: slideOut 0.28s ease-out forwards; }
@keyframes slideIn  { from { transform: translateX(120%); opacity: 0; } to { transform: translateX(0); opacity: 1; } }
@keyframes slideOut { from { transform: translateX(0); opacity: 1; max-height: 200px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
                      to   { transform: translateX(120%); opacity: 0; max-height: 0; margin-bottom: 0; padding: 0; } }
```

**Hard cap pitfall**: don't do `setTimeout(() => oldest.remove(), 280)` for
the cap trim — that's "soft" because the toast stays visible during
the slide-out animation and the count can grow during bursts. Use
`oldest.remove()` synchronously instead. The 5th toast appears, the
6th comes in, oldest is hard-removed in the same microtask, count
stays at 5.

**Why the `dismiss(el)` inner function** instead of inlining: the
X click, the body click, and the timer all need to call the same
`dismiss` with the same `isConnected` guard (prevents the timer
from removing a node that was already removed by a click). Inner
function captures `el` in closure so the guard is per-instance.

**Three dismissable surfaces**: the X button, clicking the body
anywhere, and the auto-timer. The first two get the user's
`stopPropagation()` on X so clicking X doesn't also trigger
the body click.

## 6. Global API loading spinner with try/finally counter

The single most visible "feels professional" upgrade after toasts.
The user can see when the dashboard is talking to the API.

```js
// api wrapper with global loading spinner
let _apiCount = 0;
function _spinStart() {
  _apiCount++;
  const b = $('apiSpinner'); if (b) b.classList.add('on');
  const c = $('apiSpinnerCount'); if (c) c.textContent = _apiCount > 1 ? '×' + _apiCount : '';
}
function _spinEnd() {
  _apiCount = Math.max(0, _apiCount - 1);
  const b = $('apiSpinner'); if (b && !_apiCount) b.classList.remove('on');
  const c = $('apiSpinnerCount'); if (c) c.textContent = _apiCount > 1 ? '×' + _apiCount : '';
}

const api = {
  async get(path) {
    _spinStart();
    try { const r = await fetch(S.apiBase + path); if (!r.ok) throw new Error('GET ' + path + ' ' + r.status); return r.json(); }
    finally { _spinEnd(); }
  },
  async post(path, body) {
    _spinStart();
    try { const r = await fetch(S.apiBase + path, { method:'POST', headers:{'Content-Type':'application/json'}, body: JSON.stringify(body||{}) });
          if (!r.ok) throw new Error('POST ' + path + ' ' + r.status); return r.json(); }
    finally { _spinEnd(); }
  },
};
window.api = api;
```

```html
<div id="apiSpinner" class="api-spinner" title="API call in flight">
  <div class="spinner-ring"></div>
  <span class="spinner-count" id="apiSpinnerCount"></span>
</div>
```

```css
.api-spinner {
  position: fixed; bottom: 20px; right: 20px; z-index: 99998;
  display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;
  padding: 8px 14px 8px 10px;
  background: var(--bg-2); border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: 24px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--text-2);
  box-shadow: 0 6px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);
  opacity: 0; transform: translateY(8px);
  transition: opacity 0.2s, transform 0.2s;
  pointer-events: none;
}
.api-spinner.on { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
.api-spinner .spinner-ring {
  width: 16px; height: 16px; border: 2px solid var(--line);
  border-top-color: var(--brand-2); border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 0.8s linear infinite;
}
.api-spinner .spinner-count { font-family: 'SF Mono', monospace; font-weight: 700; color: var(--brand-2); }
@keyframes spin { to { transform: rotate(360deg); } }
```

**Two critical details**:

1. **`try/finally` for the counter, not `try/catch` + manual decrement.**
   If `fetch` throws, you MUST still decrement. The `finally` block
   runs whether the request succeeded or threw — without it, a single
   failing API call permanently pins the spinner on.

2. **Show the `×N` count ONLY when N > 1.** A single in-flight call
   shows just the spinner ring. Two or more shows "×2", "×3", etc.
   This avoids the "spin spin spin, just one call, why are you
   flashing" feeling for the common single-request case.

**Bottom-right placement** is the standard — out of the way of the
content but always in the visual periphery. The toast container is
top-right, so they don't fight for space.

## Sharing infrastructure between palette and toast

Notice that `paletteCopyText`, `paletteOpenScan`, etc. all call
`toast()`. The toast is the **single feedback channel** for
"something happened" — palette runs use it to confirm copy,
tests use it to confirm save, API calls surface errors through it
(via `api.req()`'s throw). Don't have parallel feedback systems
(alert(), confirm(), browser notifications, status bar text).
One toast, one place to look, one animation grammar.

Same for the spinner — the **single progress channel** for "the
app is doing work." If you find yourself adding a second
progress indicator (button spinner, in-modal spinner, route
loader), you're violating the principle. Use the global spinner
or the toast with `ms: 0` for persistent feedback, not new
visuals.

## Verification ladder

After wiring all five, verify in this order:
1. Trigger an API call (e.g. click a refresh button) → spinner
   appears bottom-right for the call duration, hides when done.
2. Trigger 2 parallel API calls → spinner shows "×2", then "×1",
   then hides.
3. Fire 7 toasts in a synchronous burst → exactly 5 remain
   immediately (cap working), all 5 have icon + X + progress bar.
4. Click X on a toast → it animates out, 4 remain.
5. Wait 4.5s → a toast animates out, 3 remain (timer working).
6. `⌘+K`, type `>bab` → palette shows FINDINGS + SCANS sections.
7. Click a finding → modal opens, palette closes.
8. `⌘+K`, type nothing → 5 recent commands shown with `RECENT` tag.
9. `⌘+K`, type `theme` → only the Toggle Theme action shown.
10. ⌘+5 → switches to Orchestrator tab (palette shortcut chip
    `[⌘][5]` is also visible on that row in the palette).

If any step fails, the bug is almost always in:
- Step 1-2: `try/finally` missing in the api wrapper
- Step 3: `setTimeout`-based cap trim (use sync `oldest.remove()`)
- Step 6: `if (q.startsWith('>')) return renderDataSearch(...)`
  branch missing or `>` not being captured
- Step 8: recents localStorage not being read at the start of
  `renderPalette`, or the tag only showing in search-results mode

references.references/notifications-drawer-and-pani:
# Notifications drawer + bell counter + global panic button

The class of work: persist the toast stream to a re-openable history,
increment a header counter as feedback fires, and add a single-click
"kill everything" emergency action. For agentic dashboards with
lots of background activity, a long-running user walks away, comes back
wondering "what happened while I was out?" — this is the answer.

## 1. Persistent toast history (last 50)

Toasts auto-dismiss after 4.5s; that's fine for the moment but useless
for "what did that error say again?" The drawer solves it by writing
every toast to a small ring buffer in localStorage.

```js
const TOAST_HIST_KEY = 'wehax.toastHistory';
let _toastHistory = [];
try { _toastHistory = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(TOAST_HIST_KEY) || '[]'); } catch {}

function _recordToast(t) {
  _toastHistory.unshift(t);                  // newest first
  if (_toastHistory.length > 50) _toastHistory.length = 50;   // cap at 50
  try { localStorage.setItem(TOAST_HIST_KEY, JSON.stringify(_toastHistory)); } catch {}
}

function clearToastHistory() {
  _toastHistory = [];
  try { localStorage.removeItem(TOAST_HIST_KEY); } catch {}
  renderNotificationsDrawer();
}
window.clearToastHistory = clearToastHistory;
```

Call `_recordToast` from inside `toast()` right after appending the
node — but BEFORE the auto-dismiss timer, so the record exists even
if the node gets dismissed in 4.5s:

```js
function toast(title, body, level='', ms=4500) {
  // ... existing code, appendChild(el) ...
  _recordToast({ title, body, level, ts: Date.now() });
  // ... auto-dismiss timer ...
}
```

The shape stored is intentionally minimal (`{title, body, level, ts}`)
to keep localStorage small — 50 entries × ~150 bytes = ~7KB.

## 2. The drawer markup (slide-in from right)

A 420px-wide fixed panel that slides in from the right. The backdrop
dims the rest of the page; clicking the backdrop (or Esc) closes it.

```html
<!-- inside the body, after the toast container -->
<div id="notifDrawer" class="notif-drawer">
  <div class="notif-head">
    <h5><i class="lni lni-alarm"></i> Notifications</h5>
    <div class="notif-head-actions">
      <button class="notif-clear" onclick="clearToastHistory()" title="Clear all">
        <i class="lni lni-trash"></i> Clear</button>
      <button class="notif-close" onclick="closeNotifDrawer()" title="Close">
        <i class="lni lni-close"></i></button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="notifList" class="notif-list"></div>
</div>
<div id="notifBackdrop" class="notif-backdrop" onclick="closeNotifDrawer()"></div>
```

```css
.notif-backdrop { position: fixed; inset: 0; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.6);
  z-index: 99990; opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; transition: opacity 0.25s;
  backdrop-filter: blur(2px); }
