# Keyboard shortcuts + help overlay

The class of work: add a discoverable keyboard shortcut system to a
vanilla-JS dashboard with a modal-driven help overlay that lists
every binding. Targets: agentic / scan / admin dashboards, not
marketing sites.

## The three shortcut classes

Most useful keymaps blend three styles:

1. **Plain keys** for the most-used actions, no modifier needed.
   `?` for help. `Esc` to close. `/` to focus search.
2. **⌘/Ctrl chords** for power actions. `⌘K` to focus search
   when `/` is too discoverable. `⌘N` for new. `⌘/` for toggle.
3. **Vim-style `g` prefix** for tab navigation. `g` then `d`
   (Dashboard), `f` (Findings), `s` (Subdomains), etc. within
   1 second of each other. Cheap to add, fast to use.

A single document-level `keydown` listener handles all three.
Skip the listener when the user is typing in an
`input`/`textarea`/`contenteditable` so existing keystrokes
(space, escape, enter, arrow keys) still work normally.

## Full implementation

```js
document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  const tag = (e.target?.tagName || '').toLowerCase();
  if (tag === 'input' || tag === 'textarea' || e.target?.isContentEditable) return;
  const cmd = e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey;

  // ── globals ──
  if (e.key === '?' || (e.shiftKey && e.key === '/')) {
    e.preventDefault(); openHelp(); return;
  }
  if (e.key === 'Escape') { closeAllModals(); return; }
  if (cmd && e.key === 'k') { e.preventDefault(); $('searchBox')?.focus(); return; }
  if (cmd && e.key === 'n') { e.preventDefault(); openModal('modal-newscan'); return; }
  if (cmd && e.key === '/') {
    e.preventDefault();
    document.getElementById('themeBtn')?.click();
    return;
  }

  // ── vim-style prefix: g then letter within 1s ──
  if (e.key === 'g') {
    window.__gPrefix = true;
    setTimeout(() => { window.__gPrefix = false; }, 1000);
    return;
  }
  if (window.__gPrefix) {
    const map = {
      d: 'home', l: 'live', f: 'findings', s: 'subdomains',
      o: 'orchestrator', r: 'system', e: 'settings', m: 'memory',
    };
    if (map[e.key]) {
      e.preventDefault();
      setTab(map[e.key]);
      window.__gPrefix = false;
      return;
    }
  }
});

function closeAllModals() {
  document.querySelectorAll('.wehax-modal.show').forEach(m => m.classList.remove('show'));
}

function openHelp() { openModal('modal-help'); }
window.openHelp = openHelp;        // expose for onclick attr fallback
```

## Help overlay modal markup

A 2-column grid of `<kbd>` elements. Mobile collapses to 1 column
via `@media (max-width: 576px)`.

```html
<div class="wehax-modal" id="modal-help"><div class="box" style="max-width:780px">
  <h5><i class="lni lni-keyboard"></i> Keyboard Shortcuts</h5>
  <p style="color:var(--text-2);font-size:12.5px;margin-bottom:18px">
    Master these to fly through WeHax without ever touching the mouse.
  </p>
  <div class="help-grid">
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Toggle theme</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>/</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Focus search</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>K</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">New scan</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>N</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Close modal / panel</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>Esc</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Show this help</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>?</kbd></span>
    </div>

    <div class="help-divider">Navigation (press <kbd>G</kbd> then key)</div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Dashboard</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Live Feed</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>L</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Verified Findings</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>F</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Subdomains</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Orchestrator</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>O</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Resources</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>R</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Settings</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>E</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Memory</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>M</kbd></span>
    </div>

    <div class="help-divider">Constellation zoom</div>
    <div class="help-row">
      <span class="help-label">Zoom in / out / reset</span>
      <span class="help-keys"><kbd>+</kbd> <kbd>−</kbd> <kbd>0</kbd></span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="d-flex justify-content-end mt-3">
    <button class="btn btn-outline btn-sm" onclick="closeModal('modal-help')">Close</button>
  </div>
</div></div>
```

## CSS — kbd + grid + responsive

```css
.help-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 10px 28px; }
.help-row {
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
  padding: 8px 12px;
  background: var(--bg-0); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px;
}
.help-row .help-label { color: var(--text); font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 500; }
.help-row .help-keys { display: inline-flex; gap: 4px; align-items: center; }
.help-row kbd, .help-divider kbd {
  display: inline-block; min-width: 22px; padding: 3px 7px;
  font-size: 10.5px; font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--text); background: var(--bg-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--line); border-bottom-width: 2px;
  border-radius: 4px; font-family: 'SF Mono', monospace;
}
.help-divider {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
  color: var(--text-3); font-size: 10px; text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1.2px; font-weight: 700;
  padding: 14px 0 4px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); margin-top: 6px;
}
.help-divider:first-of-type { border-top: 0; padding-top: 4px; }
@media (max-width: 576px) {
  .help-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; }
}
```

The kbd design — raised 2px bottom border, monospace font, tight
padding — reads as "physical key" on both light and dark themes
without any extra rules. No per-theme overrides needed.

## Discovery — a header button for the help overlay

Most users won't press `?` until they're shown. Add a `?` icon
button to the header next to the theme button:

```html
<button class="icon-btn" id="helpBtn" title="Keyboard shortcuts (?)">
  <i class="lni lni-question-circle"></i>
</button>
```

```js
on('helpBtn', openHelp);
```

The icon (`lni-question-circle` in LineIcons, `bi-question-circle` in
Bootstrap Icons) is the universal "help" affordance. Tooltip should
hint at the keyboard shortcut too.

## Pitfalls

1. **`?` and `/` differ across keyboard layouts.** US layouts produce
   `e.key === '?'` directly when Shift is held. Many others produce
   `e.key === '/'` with `e.shiftKey === true`. Match both:
   `if (e.key === '?' || (e.shiftKey && e.key === '/'))`.

2. **`Esc` should be the universal "close whatever is open."** If you
   have nested modals, `closeAllModals()` is the safe primitive —
   iterate `.wehax-modal.show` and remove the class. Don't try to
   track which modal opened which.

3. **Don't hijack `Tab`.** The browser uses it for focus traversal.
   Same for `Space` (scrolls), `ArrowUp/Down` (cursor / scroll),
   `Enter` (form submit). Restrict your listener to letters,
   numbers, `?`, and modifier chords.

4. **The `g` prefix timer state is on `window`** so the second keydown
   in the same pair can read it. Using a local `let` inside the
   listener doesn't work — each event creates a new binding.
   `window.__gPrefix` is the simple, debuggable option.

5. **Test with a real `keydown`, not `keypress` or `keyup`.** `keypress`
   is deprecated and doesn't fire for non-character keys. `keyup`
   fires after the user releases — feels laggy. `keydown` is the
   right choice for interactive shortcuts.

6. **Don't preventDefault on keys you're not handling.** If a future
   user adds a global `/` shortcut and you call `preventDefault()`
   in a catch-all, you'll break it. Only call `preventDefault()` on
   keys you actually consume.

7. **Help button discoverability vs. help modal discoverability.**
   The header `?` button teaches the keyboard shortcut. The keyboard
   shortcut (pressing `?`) shows the same overlay. Two paths to the
   same destination is correct.

references.references/list-to-cards-and-polish-laye:
# List-heavy panes → cards, header overflow menu, and polish layers

Captured from the WeHax round where the user kept saying "keep continue",
"make the layouts more creative", and "keep gooning" — i.e. push polish
further on a dashboard that already worked. These are the patterns that
came after the core bento conversion (see `references/bento-grid-layout.md`).

## 1. List-heavy pane → responsive card grid (Subdomains pattern)

A flat monospace `<div>` list of hosts is the boring default. Convert to
a `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(240px, 1fr))` card grid with a filter input
and a grid/list view toggle. Each card: a colored left accent bar (`::before`),
a glowing status dot, the value (monospace, ellipsis-truncated), an optional
source tag, a copy-on-click icon revealed on hover.

```css
.sub-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 10px; }
.sub-card { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:11px; padding:12px 14px;
  background:var(--bg-2); border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:10px;
  position:relative; overflow:hidden; cursor:pointer; transition:all .16s ease; }
.sub-card::before { content:''; position:absolute; left:0; top:0; bottom:0; width:3px; background:var(--cyan); opacity:.6; }
.sub-card:hover { transform:translateY(-2px); border-color:var(--cyan); box-shadow:0 6px 18px rgba(0,0,0,.3); }
.sub-card .sub-host { flex:1; min-width:0; font-family:'SF Mono',monospace; white-space:nowrap; overflow:hidden; text-overflow:ellipsis; }
```

The JS keeps a `_subView = 'grid'|'list'` module-level var, a `setSubView(v)`
that toggles the active class + re-renders, and a `subHostOf(s)` coercion
helper. **Pitfall:** the host value can be a string OR an object with
`.subdomain`/`.target`/`.host` — coerce with
`String(s.subdomain || s.target || s.host || (typeof s==='string'?s:'') || '')`
or `.slice`/`.toLowerCase` on a stray object throws
`n.label.slice is not a function`. Click-to-copy uses
`navigator.clipboard?.writeText(h)` with a toast (and a warn toast on the
insecure-origin failure path).

## 2. Severity stat-strip as clickable quick-filter (Findings pattern)

Above a findings table, a row of 6 colored chips (critical/high/medium/low/
info/all) doubles as a one-click severity filter. Each chip: big number +
uppercase label, color-coded via a `--c` custom prop, left accent bar,
corner radial glow, `.active` highlight.

```css
.sev-strip { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(6,1fr); gap:10px; margin-bottom:18px; }
.sev-chip { position:relative; padding:14px 12px; border-radius:12px; cursor:pointer;
  border:1px solid var(--line); background:var(--bg-2); transition:all .18s ease; overflow:hidden; }
.sev-chip::before { content:''; position:absolute; left:0; top:0; bottom:0; width:3px; background:var(--c,var(--text-3)); }
.sev-chip.active { border-color:var(--c); background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--c) 12%, var(--bg-2)); }
.sev-chip.critical{--c:var(--red)} .sev-chip.high{--c:var(--orange)} .sev-chip.medium{--c:var(--amber)}
.sev-chip.low{--c:var(--cyan)} .sev-chip.info{--c:var(--green)} .sev-chip.all{--c:var(--brand-2)}
```

```js
function quickSev(sev) { const sel=$('fSev'); if(sel) sel.value=sev; renderFindings(); }
function updateSevStrip() { /* count verifiedOnly(S.findings) per severity, set chip numbers, toggle .active to match fSev */ }
```

**Pitfall that bit me:** `quickSev('info')` silently did nothing because the
`<select id="fSev">` had options for critical/high/medium/low but **not
`info`**. Setting `.value='info'` on a select with no matching `<option>`
fails silently (value stays `''`). Lesson: the quick-filter chips and the
underlying `<select>` must share the SAME option set — if a chip exists,
its value must be a real `<option>`.

## 3. Header overflow menu — declutter when icon buttons pile up

Past ~6 header icon buttons the bar gets busy. Move secondary/utility
toggles (sound, presentation mode, panic) into a single "⋯" dropdown.
Primary actions (theme, palette, help, bell) stay inline.

```html
<div class="overflow-wrap">
  <button class="icon-btn" id="overflowBtn" onclick="toggleOverflow()"><i class="lni lni-more-alt"></i></button>
  <div class="overflow-menu" id="overflowMenu">
    <button class="ovf-item" id="soundBtn" onclick="toggleSound()"><i class="lni lni-volume-mute"></i><span>New-finding sound</span></button>
    <button class="ovf-item" id="presentBtn" onclick="togglePresent()"><i class="lni lni-display"></i><span>Presentation mode</span></button>
    <div class="ovf-sep"></div>
    <button class="ovf-item danger" id="panicBtn" onclick="globalPanic()"><i class="lni lni-warning"></i><span>PANIC — stop all + wipe</span></button>
  </div>
</div>
```

`.overflow-menu { position:absolute; top:calc(100%+8px); right:0; display:none; }`
`.overflow-menu.open { display:block; }`. `toggleOverflow()` toggles `.open`;
a document `click` listener closes it when `!e.target.closest('.overflow-wrap')`.
**Keep toggle state on the menu item, not a separate header button** — the
`.on` class (purple highlight) shows active state, and the icon-swap in
`toggleSound()` targets the inner `<i>` so the `<span>` text label survives.

## 4. WebAudio chime on events (no audio files)

Severity-pitched two-oscillator chime, off by default (autoplay norms),
persisted to localStorage. Lower pitch = more severe.

```js
let _soundOn = false, _audioCtx = null;
function _chime(sev) {
  if (!_soundOn) return;
  _audioCtx = _audioCtx || new (window.AudioContext||window.webkitAudioContext)();
  const ctx = _audioCtx;
  const base = sev==='critical'?220 : sev==='high'?294 : sev==='medium'?392 : sev==='low'?523 : 659;
  [0,1].forEach(k => {
    const o=ctx.createOscillator(), g=ctx.createGain();
    o.type='sine'; o.frequency.value = base*(k?1.5:1); o.connect(g); g.connect(ctx.destination);
    const t0 = ctx.currentTime + k*0.08;
    g.gain.setValueAtTime(0,t0); g.gain.linearRampToValueAtTime(0.18, t0+0.02);
    g.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.001, t0+0.45);
    o.start(t0); o.stop(t0+0.46);
  });
}
```

Lazy-construct the `AudioContext` only after the user enables sound (a user
gesture) — constructing it on page load gives a console warning and a
suspended context.

## 5. Presentation mode — auto-cycle tabs

Toggle that `setInterval`s through the tab list every ~8s, adds a glowing
viewport border (`body.presenting::after` with `box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px`)
and a "▶ PRESENTING" badge. Stores no state; clear the interval + remove
the class on toggle-off. Good for demos / wall displays.

## 6. New-event pulse ring on a canvas node

To draw attention when a brand-new item appears in a canvas viz: diff
against a `Set` of seen ids (seeded on first build so there's no pulse
storm on initial load), stamp `node.pulseT = ASM.t` on new nodes, and in
the draw loop render an expanding fading ring while `ASM.t - n.pulseT < 2.4`:

```js
if (n.pulseT != null) {
  const age = ASM.t - n.pulseT;
  if (age < 2.4) {
    const p = age/2.4;
    ctx.strokeStyle = n.color + Math.round((1-p)*180).toString(16).padStart(2,'0');
    ctx.lineWidth = 2*(1-p)+0.5;
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(n.x, n.y, n.r+2 + p*26, 0, Math.PI*2); ctx.stroke();
  } else n.pulseT = null;
}
```

## 7. Ambient animated gradient mesh background

A fixed `body::before` with 4 drifting radial-gradient blobs and a slow
32s `meshDrift` keyframe (translate + scale + rotate, `alternate`). Sits at
`z-index:0`; bump `.main-wrapper`/`.sidebar` to `z-index:1` so content stays
above. Dim to ~0.6 opacity in light mode. Always gate with
`@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { body::before { animation:none; } }`.

## 8. Skeleton shimmer while first data loads

Paint shimmer rows into list containers at the very TOP of `boot()` (before
any data lands), replaced automatically when the first render runs:

```js
const sk = (n,tall) => `<div class="skeleton">${Array.from({length:n}).map(()=>`<div class="sk-row${tall?' tall':''}"></div>`).join('')}</div>`;
['recentFindingsList','findingsList'].forEach(id => { const e=$(id); if(e&&!e.children.length) e.innerHTML=sk(4); });
```

`.sk-row { background:linear-gradient(100deg,var(--bg-2) 30%,var(--bg-3) 50%,var(--bg-2) 70%); background-size:220% 100%; animation:skShimmer 1.4s ease-in-out infinite; }`

## 9. Functional regression sweep after big layout refactors (DO THIS)

After converting many panes to bento/card grids, the markup moved a lot —
verify nothing broke functionally before calling it done. One console probe
checks every interactive surface at once:

```js
(function(){
  const fns = ['openModal','closeModal','openPalette','openGlobalSearch','openNotifDrawer',
    'openRelGraph','renderFindings','quickSev','setSubView','copySub','renderMemory',
    'toggleSound','togglePresent','toggleOverflow','openFindingModal','openNewScan','launchNewScan'];
  const missingFns = fns.filter(f => typeof window[f] !== 'function');
  const ids = ['asmCanvas','findingsList','subsList','memoryList','modal-newscan','overflowMenu','sevStrip'];
  const missingIds = ids.filter(id => !document.getElementById(id));
  return JSON.stringify({ missingFns, missingIds, errors: window.__WEHAX_ERRORS||[] });
})()
```

Then drive the real paths (open each modal, click a finding → detail modal,
switch all tabs, constellation zoom in/out). The user's standard for this
project: **"run all features" = end-to-end browser verification that each
feature visibly works**, not code that "should" work. Launch flows
(New Scan) get verified by checking the API actually created the resource
(`curl /scans` count went up, status=running) — not just that the modal
closed.

references.references/timeline-sparkline.md:
# Findings timeline sparkline (canvas, stacked bars by severity)

The class of work: show a compact, time-bucketed "how busy has the
scanner been" indicator that fits under the hero on the dashboard
home. Zero deps, ~50 lines of canvas, theme-aware.

## Why a sparkline, not a counter

The home already has 4 stat cards. Adding a 5th counter ("verified
findings this hour: 3") would just be another number. A sparkline
shows the **shape** of recent activity — bursts, lulls, ramp-up,
shutdown — at a glance. The user sees "we're in a busy burst" or
"nothing's happened in 40 minutes" without reading the number.

## HTML

Drop a canvas under the hero. Wrap it in a container with a header
+ count + legend so the user knows what the colors mean.

```html
<div class="hero-timeline" id="heroTimeline">
  <div class="tl-header">
    <span class="tl-title">FINDINGS TIMELINE</span>
    <span class="tl-count" id="tlCount">0 total · last hour</span>
  </div>
  <canvas id="tlCanvas" width="800" height="60"></canvas>
  <div class="tl-legend">
    <span class="tl-leg"><i style="background:var(--red)"></i>critical</span>
    <span class="tl-leg"><i style="background:var(--orange)"></i>high</span>
    <span class="tl-leg"><i style="background:var(--amber)"></i>medium</span>
    <span class="tl-leg"><i style="background:var(--cyan)"></i>low</span>
    <span class="tl-leg"><i style="background:var(--green)"></i>info</span>
  </div>
</div>
```

## CSS

```css
.hero-timeline { margin-top: 22px; padding: 12px 14px;
  background: rgba(10,13,20,0.5); border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: 10px; }
.tl-header { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
  margin-bottom: 6px; }
.tl-title { color: var(--text-3); font-size: 9.5px; text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 1.5px; font-weight: 800; }
.tl-count  { color: var(--brand-2); font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700;
  font-family: 'SF Mono', monospace; }
#tlCanvas  { width: 100%; height: 60px; display: block; border-radius: 6px; }
.tl-legend { display: flex; gap: 14px; margin-top: 6px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
.tl-leg { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 4px;
  color: var(--text-3); font-size: 10px; font-weight: 600; }
.tl-leg i { width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; display: inline-block; }
```

The container background uses `rgba(10,13,20,0.5)` (semi-transparent
dark) so it works against both the dark and light hero gradients.
On light theme, this looks like a slightly darker inset panel — fine,
it stands out from the white hero card behind it.

## JS — the renderer

Twelve time buckets, each 5 minutes wide, totaling 1 hour. Verified
findings are bucketed by `found_at` (or `timestamp` fallback, or
"now" if neither exists, which dumps unknown-timestamps into the
rightmost bucket).

```js
function renderTimeline() {
  const cv = $('tlCanvas');
  if (!cv) return;
  const dpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
  const W = cv.offsetWidth || 800;
  const H = 60;
  cv.width = W * dpr; cv.height = H * dpr;            // crisp on retina
  const ctx = cv.getContext('2d');
  ctx.scale(dpr, dpr);
  const vList = verifiedOnly(S.findings);              // SAME discipline as everywhere

  // 12 time buckets, 5 min each, 1 hour total
  const buckets = Array(12).fill(0).map(() =>
    ({ critical:0, high:0, medium:0, low:0, info:0 }));
  const now = Date.now();
  const WINDOW = 60 * 60 * 1000;
  vList.forEach(f => {
    const ts = f.found_at ? new Date(f.found_at).getTime()
              : f.timestamp ? new Date(f.timestamp).getTime()
              : now;
    if (isNaN(ts)) return;
    const ago = now - ts;
    if (ago > WINDOW) return;
    const idx = Math.max(0, 11 - Math.floor(ago / (WINDOW/12)));
    const sev = (f.severity || 'info').toLowerCase();
    if (['critical','high','medium','low','info'].includes(sev)) buckets[idx][sev]++;
  });

  const total = vList.length;
  const cnt = $('tlCount');
  if (cnt) cnt.textContent = total + ' total · last hour';

  // Scale bars to the max bucket (so empty buckets are 0-height,
  // the busiest bucket fills the canvas height)
  let max = 0;
  buckets.forEach(b => { Object.values(b).forEach(v => { if (v > max) max = v; }); });
  if (!max) max = 1;

  // Hardcoded colors are fine here — the legend dots are also hardcoded,
  // and the dashboard's --red/--orange/--amber/--cyan/--green don't need
  // to be referenced in canvas (canvas doesn't read CSS vars by name).
  const colors = { critical:'#ff5d6c', high:'#ff8c5a', medium:'#fbbf24',
                   low:'#38e8ff', info:'#4ade80' };

  // grid lines (very subtle)
  ctx.strokeStyle = 'rgba(255,255,255,0.05)';
  ctx.lineWidth = 1;
  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    const y = (H / 4) * i + 0.5;
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(0, y); ctx.lineTo(W, y); ctx.stroke();
  }

  // Stacked bars per bucket: info on bottom, critical on top
  const colW = W / 12;
  buckets.forEach((b, i) => {
    const x = i * colW + 2;
    const w = colW - 4;
    let stackY = H - 2;
    ['info','low','medium','high','critical'].forEach(sev => {
      const v = b[sev];
      if (!v) return;
      const h = (v / max) * (H - 4);
      stackY -= h;
      ctx.fillStyle = colors[sev];
      ctx.fillRect(x, stackY, w, h);
    });
  });

  // Subtle purple gradient overlay (composited as 'screen' so it
  // tints the bars without obscuring them)
  const grd = ctx.createLinearGradient(0, 0, 0, H);
  grd.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(124,92,255,0.15)');
  grd.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(124,92,255,0)');
  ctx.fillStyle = grd;
  ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'screen';
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);
  ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';
}
window.renderTimeline = renderTimeline;
```

Call it from `loadHero()` after the scan data is fetched:

```js
async function loadHero() {
  if (!S.scanId) return;
  try {
    const s = await api.get('/scans/' + S.scanId);
    S.currentScan = s;
    S.findings = s.findings || [];
    // ... existing hero updates ...
    renderTimeline();         // <-- add
  } catch(e) {}
}
```

## Five things that matter

1. **`devicePixelRatio` scaling** — without `cv.width = W * dpr`, the
   canvas looks blurry on retina. The `ctx.scale(dpr, dpr)` at the
   start of the renderer means the rest of the code can keep using
   logical pixels.

2. **`verifiedOnly(S.findings)`** — the same discipline applied to
   every other surface. The timeline MUST NOT show unverified
   findings; the user explicitly said "don't show vuln unless it's
   verified." This applies to the sparkline, the donut, the
   constellation, AND the timeline.

3. **Buckets are right-to-left in time** — bucket[0] is 60 min ago,
   bucket[11] is "just now". `ago > WINDOW` skips old findings,
   `Math.max(0, 11 - Math.floor(ago / (WINDOW/12)))` computes the
   right index. `now` fallback (for missing timestamps) puts the
   finding in bucket[11] = "now."

4. **Stacked bars with fixed ordering** (info → low → medium → high →
   critical, bottom to top) means the user always reads the worst
   color on top — even if the eye picks up just the topmost pixel,
   the severity is correct.

5. **`globalCompositeOperation = 'screen'`** on the gradient overlay
   tints the bars without darkening the gaps. Switch back to
   `'source-over'` after or the next `fillRect` will also use screen
   mode and surprise you.

## Why no `getComputedStyle` for the colors

The toast, palette items, sidebar activity pulse — all theme-aware
via CSS vars. The timeline colors are hardcoded hex. Why?

- The severity colors are **semantic**, not theme-derived. A
  `critical` finding is red whether the dashboard is dark or light.
- The legend dots are also hardcoded — if the canvas were theme-aware
  but the legend were not, they'd disagree.
- Canvas can't read CSS vars by name anyway; you'd be using
  `getComputedStyle(documentElement).getPropertyValue('--red')` per
  frame (cheap, but pointlessly). The `--red` var is itself derived
  from a hardcoded hex.

Trade-off: if you change the brand palette via the theme picker,
the timeline doesn't follow. Accept that, or wire the canvas to
read the vars like the constellation does (see `theme-and-responsive.md`
§ "Canvas code that respects the theme").

## Verification ladder

After wiring the timeline:

1. **Shape shows busy/quiet** — find a scan with 5+ findings in
   the last hour; verify the bar at the right edge is taller than
   the left. Find one with no recent activity; verify the right
   edge is empty.
2. **Severity stacking** — inject test findings of all 5 severities
   in bucket 11; verify you see green at the bottom, cyan, amber,
   orange, red stacked upward.
3. **Re-render on every loadHero** — set S.findings to `[]` in the
   console, then call `renderTimeline()`; verify the bars disappear
   and the count reads "0 total · last hour".
4. **DPR scaling** — on a retina display (devicePixelRatio 2 or 3),
   the bars should be sharp, not blurry. Check by zooming the
   screenshot.
5. **Verified-only filter** — inject an unverified finding
   (`f.verified = 0`); it MUST NOT appear in any bucket.
6. **Resizes with viewport** — resize the window from 1280px to
   768px; the canvas re-flows and the bars redistribute without
   overflow or gaps.

## When to skip this pattern

- The app has no concept of "events over time" (CRUD, settings, reports)
- Findings are extremely rare (the user never has more than 1-2 per
  session — the timeline would be empty 99% of the time)
- The home already has a different "activity" widget that does this
  job (e.g. the sidebar progress rows from `sidebar-progress-rows.md`)

The timeline complements the sidebar progress rows — one shows
"how's the work going" (current), the other shows "how busy has
the system been" (recent). Different signals, different surfaces.

references.references/round19-modal-theme-and-outsi:
# Round 19 — Modal theme polish + outside-click-to-close

User asked: *"beuty te design in light mode also on dark mode also, allow the
modal to close when click out side the widow"*. The prior modal styles
(round 10/13) were dark-only — light mode just rendered the same `var(--bg-2)`
flat card with no polish. Outside-click-to-close was not implemented (only
Esc + the per-modal Close button). This round fixes both for the 9
`.wehax-modal` instances in `web/dashboard.html` (newscan, allscans, payloads,
templates, wordlists, finding, leaks, nextmove, palette, help).

## 1. Theme-aware modal box — the CSS pattern

The single `var(--bg-2)` box looked identical in both themes because
`--bg-2` resolves to `#ffffff` in light mode (boring white card on
light slate backdrop). Replace it with **three layered rules**:

```css
/* base — works in any theme via the var() cascade */
.wehax-modal .box {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 720px;
  max-height: 80vh;
  overflow-y: auto;
  padding: 28px 28px 24px;
  border-radius: 16px;
  color: var(--text);
  background: var(--bg-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-strong), 0 0 0 1px rgba(124,92,255,0.08) inset;
  animation: modalPop 0.28s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
}
@keyframes modalPop {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px) scale(0.97); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0)    scale(1); }
}

/* gradient accent bar at the top — purple→cyan→light-purple */
.wehax-modal .box::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; height: 3px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--brand) 0%, var(--cyan) 50%, var(--brand-2) 100%);
  border-radius: 16px 16px 0 0;
  opacity: 0.85;
}

/* dark mode — deeper gradient, purple inner ring, layered shadow */
html:not(.light) .wehax-modal .box {
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(20,22,40,0.96) 0%, rgba(15,17,32,0.98) 100%);
  border-color: rgba(124,92,255,0.20);
  box-shadow: 0 30px 80px rgba(0,0,0,0.65), 0 0 60px rgba(124,92,255,0.10) inset;
}
html:not(.light) .wehax-modal { background: rgba(2,4,12,0.78); }

/* light mode — clean white→off-white with subtle purple border */
html.light .wehax-modal {
  background: rgba(15,23,42,0.32);
  backdrop-filter: blur(8px) saturate(140%);
}
html.light .wehax-modal .box {
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, #ffffff 0%, #fafbff 100%);
  border-color: rgba(109,58,255,0.18);
  box-shadow:
    0 30px 80px rgba(20,30,60,0.18),
    0 0 0 1px rgba(109,58,255,0.04) inset,
    0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.9) inset;
}
```

Three things to notice:
- **The accent bar is `position:absolute` at the top of `.box`** —
  doesn't need its own row in the modal markup. One `::before`
  element, no JS, no extra DOM.
- **The dark and light boxes use the SAME `var(--brand)` /
  `var(--cyan)` / `var(--brand-2)` CSS variables** that the rest of
  the app uses (see round 13's `--brand: #6d3aff`, `--cyan: #0aa9c0`).
  No theme-specific hardcoded hex in the bar — it just inherits.
- **Backdrop blur differs**: 10px in dark, 8px+140% saturate in light.
  Light needs a stronger saturate because the white page content
  bleeds through otherwise.

## 2. The × close button — markup change for all 9 modals

The original modal headers were bare `<h5><i class="lni lni-zap"></i> Title</h5>`.
That single inline-icon approach can't host a right-aligned close button.
**Wrap the title text in a `<span>` so the close button can sit next to it
via flexbox**:

```html
<!-- before -->
<h5><i class="lni lni-zap"></i> Launch New Scan</h5>

<!-- after -->
<h5><span><i class="lni lni-zap"></i> Launch New Scan</span>
    <button class="modal-close-x" type="button" aria-label="Close" data-modal-close>×</button></h5>
```

The CSS:
```css
.wehax-modal h5 {
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
  margin: 4px 0 18px; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: -0.01em;
}
.wehax-modal .modal-close-x {
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  width: 28px; height: 28px; border-radius: 8px;
  background: transparent; border: 1px solid transparent;
  color: var(--text-3); cursor: pointer; font-size: 16px;
  flex: 0 0 auto; margin-left: 12px;
  transition: background 0.15s, color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s, transform 0.15s;
}
.wehax-modal .modal-close-x:hover {
  background: rgba(225,29,72,0.10);
  color: var(--red);
  border-color: rgba(225,29,72,0.30);
  transform: rotate(90deg);    /* micro-delight: spin on hover */
}
```

The `data-modal-close` attribute is the hook the delegated click handler
uses (see #3). Apply the same `<span>`+button wrap to all 9 modal
headers. A find-and-replace is the right tool.

## 3. Outside-click-to-close + × handler — one delegated listener

The naive per-modal approach (each modal adds its own `onclick="if(event.target===this)close()"` to the wrapper, or attaches a per-instance `mousedown` listener) doubles in size every time a modal is added. **Use a single document-level delegated listener**:

```js
document.addEventListener('mousedown', (e) => {
  // 1) explicit × close buttons — works on every modal
  const x = e.target.closest('[data-modal-close]');
  if (x) {
    const modal = x.closest('.wehax-modal');
    if (modal) { closeModal(modal.id); e.stopPropagation(); return; }
  }
  // 2) backdrop click — only if click is OUTSIDE the modal's content box / shell
  const backdrop = e.target.closest('.wehax-modal');
  if (!backdrop || !backdrop.classList.contains('show')) return;
  if (backdrop.dataset.noBackdropClose !== undefined) return;
  // find the visible content node (regular modals use .box, palette uses .palette-shell)
  const content = backdrop.querySelector(':scope > .box, :scope > .palette-shell');
  if (content && content.contains(e.target)) return;   // click inside content → keep open
  closeModal(backdrop.id);
}, true);  // capture phase so we run before any inner handlers
```

Five things to know:
- **`mousedown` not `click`** — `mousedown` fires before focus/select
  side effects, so the user doesn't see a brief "select inside the
  input" flash before the close.
- **Capture phase (`true` as third arg)** — runs BEFORE any bubbling
  handler on a child. Stops a form input's `mousedown` from doing
  focus-prevent things. (For close-on-× we still `stopPropagation()`
  so a parent doesn't also see the close as a backdrop click — the
  `return` after `closeModal(modal.id)` handles that.)
- **`backdrop.classList.contains('show')` check** — if no modal is
  open, the document handler returns immediately. Negligible perf
  cost, but it keeps the semantic clean: "backdrop click" only
  means anything when a modal is actually showing.
- **`dataset.noBackdropClose`** — opt-out flag for the rare modal
  that needs to be persistent (none yet, but the escape hatch
  exists; add `data-no-backdrop-close` to the `.wehax-modal` to
  disable outside-click close for that one).
- **The `:scope >` selector handles the palette's nested structure
  correctly** — the command palette is `<div class="wehax-modal" id="modal-palette" data-palette><div class="palette-shell">...`. The `.palette-shell` is a direct child of `.wehax-modal`, and `:scope > .box, :scope > .palette-shell` finds it. Clicks inside the shell keep the palette open; clicks outside close it. Verified live.

## 4. Lock body scroll while a modal is open

The previous code didn't lock scroll. A 700px modal on a 5000px-tall
findings list would let the user scroll the page behind it, which
fights with the modal's intent. **Lock body scroll for the lifetime
of any open modal**:

```js
function openModal(id) {
  const m = $(id);
  if (!m) return;
  m.classList.add('show');
  document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
  // focus first focusable input/button inside, if any
  setTimeout(() => {
    const f = m.querySelector('input, select, textarea, button:not(.modal-close-x)');
    if (f && f !== document.activeElement) try { f.focus({ preventScroll: true }); } catch(_) {}
  }, 60);
}
function closeModal(id) {
  const m = $(id);
  if (!m) return;
  m.classList.remove('show');
  if (!document.querySelector('.wehax-modal.show')) document.body.style.overflow = '';
}
function closeAllModals() {
  document.querySelectorAll('.wehax-modal.show').forEach(m => m.classList.remove('show'));
  document.body.style.overflow = '';
}
```

The `if (!document.querySelector('.wehax-modal.show'))` guard is
critical: if a *second* modal is open and you close the first, you
must NOT restore body scroll — the second modal still needs it locked.
Only restore when the LAST modal closes.

## 5. Verification ladder (browser_console.evaluate, no vision needed)

The vision backend was out of credits this round (`Insufficient credits`).
Computed-style queries cover the design verification. Paste each into
`browser_console.evaluate` after `browser_navigate("http://127.0.0.1:8777/?v=N")`:

```js
// A. modal opens with new design
openModal('modal-newscan');
const m = document.getElementById('modal-newscan');
const box = m.querySelector('.box');
({
  modal_visible: m.classList.contains('show'),
  close_button_present: !!m.querySelector('.modal-close-x'),
  body_overflow: document.body.style.overflow,        // expect 'hidden'
  backdrop_bg: getComputedStyle(m).backgroundColor,   // dark/light variant
  box_bg: getComputedStyle(box).backgroundImage,      // linear-gradient(...)
  box_radius: getComputedStyle(box).borderRadius,     // 16px
})

// B. backdrop click closes (programmatic mousedown on the .wehax-modal element)
m.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('mousedown', { bubbles: true }));
({ closed: !m.classList.contains('show'), body_overflow: document.body.style.overflow })
// expect closed: true, body_overflow: ''

// C. click inside .box keeps open
openModal('modal-newscan');
box.querySelector('input').dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('mousedown', { bubbles: true }));
({ after_click_inside: m.classList.contains('show') })  // expect true

// D. × click closes
document.querySelector('#modal-newscan .modal-close-x')
  .dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('mousedown', { bubbles: true }));
({ after_x_click: m.classList.contains('show') })      // expect false

// E. palette (uses .palette-shell, not .box) works the same way
openModal('modal-palette');
const pal = document.getElementById('modal-palette');
document.querySelector('#modal-palette input').dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('mousedown', { bubbles: true }));
({ after_input_inside: pal.classList.contains('show') })  // expect true
pal.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('mousedown', { bubbles: true }));
({ after_backdrop: pal.classList.contains('show') })      // expect false

// F. light mode — toggle html.light and re-query
document.documentElement.classList.add('light');
openModal('modal-newscan');
({
  box_bg: getComputedStyle(box).backgroundImage,  // expect white→#fafbff
  box_shadow_starts: getComputedStyle(box).boxShadow.substring(0, 50),
  h5_color: getComputedStyle(box.querySelector('h5 > span')).color,  // expect dark
})
```

The full live test (4 modals × {open, click-inside, backdrop, ×} ×
{dark, light} = 32 cases) takes about 2 minutes. All 32 pass after
this round's changes.

## 6. Anti-patterns NOT to do

- **Don't use a per-modal `onclick="if(event.target===this)closeX()"`
  on the wrapper** — it doesn't compose with modals that contain
  clickable children with their own handlers. The delegated
  document listener handles all of them uniformly.
- **Don't bind `keydown` Escape per modal** — the existing single
  `keydown` listener (round 16) calls `closeAllModals()` and that's
  it. The new outside-click works alongside Esc without
  re-registering anything.
- **Don't add a 9th CSS rule per modal** — the theme-aware styles
  use `:not(.light)` / `.light` selectors that target ALL
  `.wehax-modal` instances in one block. Adding per-modal overrides
  breaks the moment a new modal is introduced.
- **Don't use `backdrop-filter: blur(12px)` in light mode** — the
  text underneath bleeds through too much and looks like a frosted
  glass museum piece. 8px + 140% saturate is the sweet spot for
  white pages. 10px works for dark because the dark backdrop hides
  the bleed.

## 7. File touchpoints

- `web/dashboard.html` — modal CSS (lines ~533-620) + 9× modal header
  `<h5>` markup wrap with `<span>` + close button
- `web/static/dashboard.js` — `openModal` / `closeModal` / `closeAllModals`
  add body-scroll lock + autofocus, plus the one document-level
  `mousedown` delegated listener

No backend changes. No `core/api.py` changes. No new endpoints.

references.references/command-palette.md:
# Command Palette (⌘+K / Ctrl+K) — full pattern

VSCode-style command palette: an input-driven overlay that fuzzy-searches
across tabs, modals, and actions, with keyboard nav (↑↓↵) and click-to-run.
The killer feature for any non-trivial dashboard. Once users discover it,
they use it for everything — it's faster than clicking nav, works on mobile
where nav is collapsed, and surfaces actions the user didn't know existed.

## Trigger

Add a ⌘+K command palette when:
- The dashboard has ≥8 navigation targets (tabs, modals, actions) — below
  that, the modal-cycles-helpful is overkill
- The user has power-user traits (you can guess from their other asks:
  "add keyboard shortcuts", "make it more efficient", "add a search bar
  for actions")
- The mobile sidebar collapses to icon-only and users lose text labels —
  the palette brings them back

Skip when:
- The app has <5 navigation targets
- The user is non-technical and unlikely to learn shortcuts

## The COMMANDS array

Single source of truth, plain JS array of objects, each with icon + label
+ sub-description + action + search keywords:

```js
const COMMANDS = [
  // tabs
  { id:'tab-home',   icon:'lni-dashboard',     label:'Dashboard',     sub:'home view',           action: () => setTab('home'),         kw:'home dashboard overview' },
  { id:'tab-live',   icon:'lni-rss-alt',       label:'Live Feed',     sub:'realtime events',     action: () => setTab('live'),         kw:'live events feed realtime stream' },
  { id:'tab-find',   icon:'lni-warning',       label:'Verified Findings', sub:'explore & inspect', action: () => setTab('findings'),     kw:'findings vulnerabilities results' },
  { id:'m-new',      icon:'lni-plus',          label:'New Scan',      sub:'launch a scan',       action: () => openModal('modal-newscan'), kw:'new scan start create launch' },
  { id:'a-stop',     icon:'lni-close',         label:'Stop All Running Scans', sub:'kill all',       action: () => $('stopAllBtn')?.click(), kw:'stop cancel kill terminate' },
  { id:'a-wipe',     icon:'lni-trash',         label:'Wipe All Data', sub:'delete every scan',   action: () => wipeAllData(),          kw:'wipe delete reset clean data scans' },
  // ... 20+ more
];
```

Three things to get right:
- **`kw` (keywords)** — natural-language synonyms the user might type.
  "find" should match "Verified Findings" via `kw:'findings vulnerabilities
  results'`, AND "realtime" should match Live Feed via `kw:'live events
  feed realtime stream'`. Make the `kw` 5-10 words covering the obvious
  ways a user would search for this.
- **`sub`** — short context, shown under the label. "tab · home view"
  or "action · confirm first" or "toolbox · attack payloads". Helps
  users distinguish two commands with similar labels.
- **`action`** — closure capturing whatever's needed. If the action needs
  to navigate AND open a modal, do both in sequence:
  `action: () => { setTab('home'); setTimeout(() => openModal('modal-new'), 100); }`

## Fuzzy scorer (no library, 8 lines)

```js
function fuzzyScore(cmd, q) {
  q = q.toLowerCase().trim();
  if (!q) return 1;                                  // empty query → show all
  const hay = (cmd.label + ' ' + cmd.sub + ' ' + (cmd.kw||'')).toLowerCase();
  if (hay.includes(q)) return 10 + (hay.startsWith(q) ? 5 : 0);  // exact / starts-with wins
  // fuzzy: every char of q must appear in hay in order
  let i = 0;
  for (const ch of hay) { if (ch === q[i]) i++; if (i === q.length) return 5; }
  return 0;
}
```

Ranking: exact substring match (10) > starts-with (15) > fuzzy-in-order (5).
Empty query returns 1 for all (sorted, capped at 12 shown).

Don't bother with a real fuzzy library (fuse.js) for ≤30 commands — the
above is fast, deterministic, and zero-dependency.

## Modal HTML — custom layout, hide the standard .box

The standard `.wehax-modal .box` is centered with rounded corners and a
backdrop. The palette wants a top-anchored, narrower, no-radius panel.
Hide the standard box and use a custom shell:

```html
<div class="wehax-modal" id="modal-palette" data-palette>
  <div class="palette-shell">
    <div class="palette-input-wrap">
      <i class="lni lni-search-alt"></i>
      <input id="paletteInput" type="text" placeholder="Type a command, tab, or action…"
             autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" />
      <span class="palette-shortcut"><kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>K</kbd></span>
    </div>
    <div id="paletteList" class="palette-list"></div>
    <div class="palette-foot">
      <span><kbd>↑</kbd><kbd>↓</kbd> navigate</span>
      <span><kbd>↵</kbd> run</span>
      <span><kbd>esc</kbd> close</span>
      <span style="margin-left:auto;color:var(--text-3)" id="paletteCount">0 commands</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

```css
.wehax-modal[data-palette] { padding: 0; align-items: flex-start; padding-top: 14vh; }
.wehax-modal[data-palette] .box { display: none; }              /* hide standard box */
.wehax-modal[data-palette] .palette-shell { display: block; ... }
```

The `data-palette` attribute lets the CSS target only the palette modal
without polluting the others.

## CSS — three layers, 30 lines total

```css
.palette-input-wrap { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;
  padding: 14px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
.palette-input-wrap input { flex: 1; background: transparent; border: 0;
  color: var(--text); font-size: 16px; outline: none; }
.palette-item { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;
  padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 8px; cursor: pointer; transition: background 0.1s; }
.palette-item.active { background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(124,92,255,0.20) 0%, rgba(56,232,255,0.10) 100%); }
.palette-item.active i { color: var(--brand-2); }
.palette-foot { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px;
  padding: 8px 16px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  background: var(--bg-0); color: var(--text-3); font-size: 10.5px; }
.palette-empty { padding: 40px 20px; text-align: center; color: var(--text-3); }
```

Light theme inherits via the same `var(--bg-2)`, `var(--line)`, etc.
No special-case rules needed.

## Open / render / run / move

```js
let _palIdx = 0;
let _palResults = [];

function openPalette() {
  renderPalette('');                       // start with all
  openModal('modal-palette');
  setTimeout(() => $('paletteInput')?.focus(), 50);   // wait for show transition
}

function renderPalette(q) {
  _palResults = COMMANDS
    .map(c => ({ c, s: fuzzyScore(c, q) }))
    .filter(r => r.s > 0)
    .sort((a,b) => b.s - a.s)
    .slice(0, 12)                           // cap at 12 visible
    .map(r => r.c);
  _palIdx = 0;
  const list = $('paletteList');
  if (!list) return;
  if (!_palResults.length) {
    list.innerHTML = '<div class="palette-empty"><i class="lni lni-search"></i> no matches for "'+esc(q)+'"</div>';
    return;
  }
  list.innerHTML = _palResults.map((c, i) => `
    <div class="palette-item ${i===0?'active':''}" data-i="${i}" onclick="paletteRun(${i})">
      <i class="${c.icon}"></i>
      <div class="palette-text">
        <div class="palette-label">${esc(c.label)}</div>
        <div class="palette-sub">${esc(c.sub||'')}</div>
      </div>
      <span class="palette-enter"><kbd>↵</kbd></span>
    </div>`).join('');
}

function paletteRun(i) {
  const c = _palResults[i];
  if (!c) return;
  closeModal('modal-palette');
  try { c.action(); } catch (e) { console.error('palette', e); }
}

function paletteMove(delta) {
  if (!_palResults.length) return;
  _palIdx = (_palIdx + delta + _palResults.length) % _palResults.length;
  document.querySelectorAll('.palette-item').forEach((el, i) =>
    el.classList.toggle('active', i === _palIdx));
  document.querySelector('.palette-item.active')?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' });
}

window.openPalette = openPalette;
window.renderPalette = renderPalette;
window.paletteRun = paletteRun;
window.paletteMove = paletteMove;

// Live filter on each keystroke
$('paletteInput')?.addEventListener('input', e => renderPalette(e.target.value));
```

## Keyboard handling — three key rules

**Rule 1: When the palette is open, the global keydown handler must yield.**

This is the most important detail. The default global handler skips
all keys when the user is typing in an input — but the palette IS an
input, so the user's ↑↓↵ keys would do nothing. Add an early-out at
the top of the global handler:

```js
document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  const tag = (e.target?.tagName || '').toLowerCase();
  const inField = tag === 'input' || tag === 'textarea' || e.target?.isContentEditable;

  // ── palette is open → handle its own keys even when typing ──
  if (document.getElementById('modal-palette')?.classList.contains('show')) {
    if (e.key === 'Escape')   { e.preventDefault(); closeModal('modal-palette'); return; }
    if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') { e.preventDefault(); paletteMove(1); return; }
    if (e.key === 'ArrowUp')   { e.preventDefault(); paletteMove(-1); return; }
    if (e.key === 'Enter')     { e.preventDefault(); paletteRun(_palIdx); return; }
  }
  if (inField) return;

  // ... rest of the global handler (?, ⌘+K, ⌘+N, g+key, etc.) ...
});
```

**Rule 2: Re-route ⌘+K from "focus search" to "open palette".**

If the global handler previously did `⌘+K → focus searchBox`, change
it to `⌘+K → openPalette()`. Update the help modal's "Focus search"
label to "Command palette" at the same time.

**Rule 3: Expose all the palette functions on `window`.**

`openPalette`, `renderPalette`, `paletteRun`, `paletteMove`, `_palIdx`
— all need to be on `window` so the browser console can test them
and so other modals can call them.

## Discoverability

The single biggest mistake is shipping ⌘+K without surfacing it. Users
won't guess. Three ways to make it discoverable:

1. **A search icon button in the header** (lni-search-alt) that opens
   the palette via `onclick="openPalette()"`. Title attribute:
   "Command palette (⌘+K)".
2. **A `⌘K` placeholder hint** in the search input across the app
   (if you have one).
3. **The help modal lists it** under "Global" with `<kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>K</kbd>`.

## Pairing with sidebars that collapse to icons on mobile

When the sidebar collapses to 64px on mobile (icon-only), the user
loses the text labels — "Dashboard", "Findings", "Settings" all
become mystery icons. The palette becomes the primary navigation on
mobile because it brings the labels back. Worth advertising this
in the help modal: "On mobile, the command palette is the fastest
way to navigate."

## Stale-page gotcha when iterating in browser tools

When iterating on the palette via `browser_console.evaluate()`, if a
prior evaluate set `window.__palIdx = 99` and the page hasn't
reloaded, that stale state can leak into later tests. Two ways to
cope:
- Always `browser_navigate(url)` with a cache-buster `?_=N` between
  test groups, then re-evaluate
- Reset the palette state explicitly at the start of each test:
  `window._palIdx = 0; openPalette();`

If `window.openPalette` returns "undefined" in a console test after
you just defined it, the page is stale (or the script failed to
parse). Reload with a cache-buster URL.

## Verification ladder

After wiring the palette, verify in this order:
1. Press `⌘+K` → modal opens, input is focused
2. Type nothing → 12 items shown, first is active
3. Type "find" → filters to 4 items, "Verified Findings" is first
4. Press ↓ → active moves from "Dashboard" to second item
5. Press ↵ → modal closes, action runs (e.g. tab changes)
6. Press `⌘+K`, type "x" → empty state shows "no matches for x"
7. Press Esc → modal closes
8. Toggle theme → palette still readable (no color hardcoded)
9. Help modal still says "Command palette" not "Focus search"

If any step fails, the bug is almost always in:
- The early-out in the global keydown handler (step 1 or 5)
- The `data-palette` CSS hiding `.box` (step 1 — modal opens but is invisible)
- The `kw` field not containing a synonym the user tried (step 3)

