The mobile sidebar expand/collapse is one class on the wrapper:
`.expanded`. The header has a `sidebarToggle` button that toggles it.
A document-level click handler closes it when the user taps outside
(only on ≤768px, so desktop clicks aren't affected).

```js
on('sidebarToggle', () => {
  document.querySelector('.sidebar-nav-wrapper')?.classList.toggle('expanded');
});
document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const sb = document.querySelector('.sidebar-nav-wrapper');
  if (!sb || !sb.classList.contains('expanded')) return;
  if (window.innerWidth > 768) return;          // only on mobile
  if (sb.contains(e.target)) return;            // click inside sidebar — ignore
  if (e.target.closest('#sidebarToggle')) return; // the toggle button itself
  sb.classList.remove('expanded');
});
```

## Canvas-rendered visualization: scale with viewport

A common bug: a constellation/graph uses fixed canvas-space coordinates
(800×380), but the canvas resizes with the container. On mobile the
inner ring (radius 120) and outer ring (radius 230) overflow the
shrunk canvas.

Fix: scale the orbit radii by the current canvas/box size:

```js
const scaleX = W / 800, scaleY = H / 380;  // W, H = current canvas px
const cx0 = 400, cy0 = 190;
ASM.nodes.forEach((n, i) => {
  if (n.ring > 0) {
    const ang = ASM.t * 0.3 + i * 0.7;
    const radius = n.ring === 1 ? 120 : 230 + (i % 3) * 8;
    n.x = cx0 + Math.cos(ang) * radius * scaleX;
    n.y = cy0 + Math.sin(ang) * (radius * 0.55) * scaleY;
  } else {
    n.x = cx0 + Math.cos(ASM.t * 0.5) * 2;
    n.y = cy0 + Math.sin(ASM.t * 0.5) * 2;
  }
});
// Wrap particles in the scaled bounds too
ASM.particles.forEach(p => {
  p.x += p.vx; p.y += p.vy;
  if (p.x < 0) p.x = 800; if (p.x > 800) p.x = 0;
  if (p.y < 0) p.y = 380; if (p.y > 380) p.y = 0;
  ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(p.x * scaleX, p.y * scaleY, p.r, 0, Math.PI * 2); ctx.fill();
});
```

This way the visualization always fits its container, whether 1280px
desktop or 360px phone, and the click-to-inspect hover math still
works (see `vanilla-js-spa` SKILL.md "Canvas: hover detection on a
transformed 2D context" for the transform-inversion recipe).

## What I had to fix mid-session (lessons)

1. **First I put overrides on `body.light` — wasted 20 minutes.** The
   variable got applied to body descendants but `getComputedStyle(html)`
   still returned the dark value. The body was light gray; the canvas
   kept drawing in dark colors. Fix: move to `html.light`.

2. **Icon class swap order matters.** I had `if (light) sun; else if
   (system) cog; else night`. In system mode both `system-theme` and
   `light` classes are present, so the first branch always won, and
   the user never saw the gear icon. **System check must come first.**

3. **`scaleX/scaleY` declared after first use.** I added the scaled
   drawing for the nodes but the particle loop above still used raw
   `p.x, p.y`. Hoist `scaleX = W/800, scaleY = H/380` to the top of
   the render function so both loops can use them.

4. **The `.collapsed` class is desktop-only.** If your desktop
   sidebar uses `.collapsed` for the hamburger and your mobile CSS
   uses `.expanded` for the same thing, your JS handler will only
   work in one mode. Pick one class name (`expanded` works for both)
   and make the mobile CSS target the same class.

5. **Body background gradient must be re-declared in light mode.**
   `body { background: radial-gradient(...rgba(124,92,255,0.10)...) }`
   uses the dark color. Override with `html.light body { background:
   radial-gradient(...rgba(109,58,255,0.06)...) }`. Otherwise the
   background "leaks" through the light cards and looks weird.

